    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The picture on the sliding-tile board.</b> The 3x3 board is opened at 3:19 and never shown with its tiles all facing the camera in a clean frame. Two passes read it as a pale image of white sacks with a scoop of grain, which is consistent but not certain, and the solved board is on screen for barely a second before the panel opens. The puzzle's mechanic is certain; the artwork is not.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. What the Battery actually powers.</b> It appears in the inventory at 7:30 and nothing in the readable footage consumes it. The red cabinet marked <b>H</b> is the obvious candidate — a battery box is exactly what an H-marked wall cabinet in a shed would be — but the recording never shows the cabinet being opened, so this page will not claim it as a step.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. What is in the red toolbox.</b> The dials and the code <b>918</b> are solid, and the lid is up at 2:12, but the contents sit under the tray and the camera angle never gives a clean look. If you are following the guide for an item, take it off the box rather than trusting a guess from this page.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. Whether the generator ever runs.</b> The plate, the switch, the pull cord and the breakers are all clearly shown and clearly worked, but the shed's lights do not come on at any point in the recording, and the darkness at 7:45 is handled by the candle instead. Either the generator is a red herring or its payoff is a door or hatch that the camera never lingers on. We are not going to invent a hidden panel for it.
    </div>
